---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import NoteIllustration from "../../../components/NoteIllustration.astro";
import NoteCover from "../../../components/NoteCover.astro";
import { SITE, notes } from "../../../data/site";

const note = notes.find((item) => item.slug === "a-working-demo-beats-a-deck")!;

const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    headline: note.title,
    description: note.summary,
    datePublished: note.date,
    author: {
      "@type": "Person",
      name: SITE.principal.name,
      url: SITE.principal.url
    },
    publisher: {
      "@type": "Organization",
      name: SITE.name,
      url: SITE.url
    }
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={note.title}
  description={note.summary}
  path={`/notes/${note.slug}/`}
  image={`/images/og/notes/${note.slug}.png`}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Notes · <time datetime={note.date}>{note.dateDisplay}</time></p>
    <h1>{note.title}</h1>
    <p>{note.summary}</p>
    <div class="note-hero-art"><NoteCover slug={note.slug} /></div>
  </section>

  <article class="article-body" data-analytics-component="article_body">
    <p>
      I had a thesis about audiobooks I couldn't get anyone to react to.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every app in the category treats AI as a feature. A synthetic voice
      here, a recommendation engine there, sitting on a catalog as fixed as
      it was in 1995. The more interesting question is what happens when the
      story itself is malleable. I said that sentence out loud a few times
      and watched people nod politely.
    </p>
    <p>
      So I built it instead. Bibo. You can open it on your phone right now.
    </p>
    <p>
      In Bibo, Moby Dick isn't a file. It's five: the classic narration, a
      shortened cut for a commute, a modern YA rewrite, a sci-fi retelling,
      and a Spanish literary translation, all fully voiced. One asset, five
      products.
    </p>

    <NoteIllustration kind="multiplier" />

    <p>
      The nodding stopped. People started arguing about which version they'd
      actually listen to, which is a much better conversation than the one
      I'd been having.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the demo works and the deck doesn't</h2>
    <p>
      A deck asks people to imagine, and everyone imagines something
      slightly different, so a room of six produces six private versions of
      your idea and one polite consensus. Nobody can picture how it feels to
      drag a slider and hear a story compress. You have to hand them the
      slider.
    </p>
    <p>
      A demo is also harder on you than a deck is. Bibo made me find out
      what AI narration sounds like across a whole book, which is not what
      it sounds like in a twenty-second sample. Some of it is worse than I'd
      have claimed on a slide. I'd rather learn that in week three than
      after someone commits real money.
    </p>

    <h2>What changed recently is the cost</h2>
    <p>
      This used to be a quarter and an agency invoice. Bibo went from
      concept to a polished, self-contained demo in weeks: AI-assisted
      build, AI narration, the translations, and more than fifty covers under
      human art direction. Which means the excuse for spending six weeks
      arguing about an abstraction is gone.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pattern generalizes past audiobooks. Any organization sitting on a
      library, a publishing backlist, a course catalog, years of research,
      is sitting on versions it has never shipped. Other lengths, other
      languages, other audiences.
    </p>
    <p class="article-cta" data-analytics-component="article_cta" data-analytics-event="cta">
      But I'd stop reading here and <a class="text-link" href="/case-studies/bibo/" data-analytics-event="recirculation">go
      open Bibo</a>. That's the whole argument.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
